        {
          "unit_id": "since_january",
          "title": "Since January",
          "description": "Cuánto tiempo llevas haciendo algo: present perfect continuous con for y since. Además, be able to y el gerundio como sujeto (Studying is hard).",
          "lessons": [
            {
              "lesson_id": "since_january_lesson",
              "title": "Present perfect continuous: for and since",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "phase": "context",
                  "type": "audio_context",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Since January",
                    "dialogue": [
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "Priya, wake up! How long have you been studying?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Priya",
                        "text": "Since six o'clock this morning. I've been preparing for my exam for weeks."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "What exam is it?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Priya",
                        "text": "It's to become a senior nurse. I've wanted this job for years."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Sam",
                        "text": "And I've been practising this song since lunchtime. Listen!"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "It sounds great, but you've been playing it all week!"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Sam",
                        "text": "That's because we've got a big gig on Saturday. A music producer is coming."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "A producer? That's amazing!"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Priya",
                        "text": "And you, Lucía? You look happy. Have you been seeing a certain Irishman?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "We've been out three times since January."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Sam",
                        "text": "Three times! Is it serious?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "Maybe. He's been teaching me Irish words. \"Grand\", \"craic\", \"eejit\"…"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Priya",
                        "text": "\"Eejit\"? That's what we call Sam!"
                      }
                    ],
                    "passage": "February. Everyone at Clifton Road seems tired. Lucía comes home from work and finds Priya asleep on her books and Sam playing the guitar in the kitchen."
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "discovery",
                  "type": "multiple_choice",
                  "content": {
                    "instructions": "Fíjate en el diálogo y responde.",
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "question": "\"How long have you been studying?\" asks about…",
                        "options": [
                          "how long until now",
                          "a finished action"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"since six o'clock\": since + …",
                        "options": [
                          "a point in time",
                          "a period of time"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"for weeks\": for + …",
                        "options": [
                          "a period of time",
                          "a point in time"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"We've been out three times.\" Why not \"have been going out three times\"?",
                        "options": [
                          "It counts how many times.",
                          "It is about duration."
                        ],
                        "answer": 0,
                        "explanation": "Cuando contamos veces o cantidades usamos el present perfect simple."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Present perfect continuous: for and since",
                    "explanation": "Have / has been + -ing describe una actividad que empezó en el pasado y continúa ahora, o que acaba de terminar y se nota: I've been studying since six. You look tired, have you been running?\n\nPara preguntar cuánto tiempo: How long have you been studying?\n\nFor + periodo (for weeks, for two hours, for years). Since + momento de inicio (since six o'clock, since January, since 2020).\n\nUsamos el present perfect simple cuando contamos (We've been out three times) y con verbos de estado (I've wanted this job for years, I've known him since September).",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Afirmativa",
                          "I've been studying since six."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Negativa",
                          "She hasn't been sleeping well."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Pregunta",
                          "How long have you been studying?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "for + periodo",
                          "for weeks, for two hours, for years"
                        ],
                        [
                          "since + momento",
                          "since six o'clock, since January, since 2020"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Cantidad → simple",
                          "We've been out three times."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Verbo de estado → simple",
                          "I've wanted this job for years."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I've been preparing for my exam for weeks.",
                      "I've been practising this song since lunchtime.",
                      "You've been playing it all week!",
                      "We've been out three times since January."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "be able to · -ing as a subject",
                    "explanation": "Can solo tiene presente (can) y pasado (could). Para los demás tiempos se usa be able to: I'll be able to buy a car (podré). I haven't been able to sleep (no he podido dormir). I'd like to be able to speak Irish.\n\nPara un logro concreto en el pasado, con esfuerzo, se usa was / were able to (no could): After three hours, I was able to finish the exam.\n\nEl gerundio (-ing) funciona como un nombre y puede ser el sujeto de la frase: Studying for exams is exhausting. Playing the guitar relaxes me. En español usamos el infinitivo («estudiar es agotador»), pero en inglés va en -ing (no «To study is…» en el habla normal).",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Ejemplo",
                        "En español"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Futuro",
                          "If I pass, I'll be able to apply for the job.",
                          "podré"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Present perfect",
                          "I haven't been able to sleep.",
                          "no he podido"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Logro en pasado",
                          "In the end, I was able to finish.",
                          "conseguí"
                        ],
                        [
                          "-ing como sujeto",
                          "Studying all day is exhausting.",
                          "estudiar todo el día"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "If Priya passes the exam, she'll be able to become a senior nurse.",
                      "Sam hasn't been able to sleep because of the gig.",
                      "Practising every day makes a big difference.",
                      "Learning Irish words is fun, but pronouncing them is hard!"
                    ]
                  }
                },
